-- No counterexample can be generated under the given schema and constraints, as the queries are semantically equivalent.
-- If the primary key (c4, c2) were removed, a counterexample could be created where a user has multiple activities on the day after their first login, causing Q1 to overcount retention.
-- For example, if table1 allowed duplicate (c4, c2):
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-01', 10, 1);
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 11, 2);
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (1, '2023-01-02', 12, 3); -- This row would violate PK
-- INSERT INTO table1 (c4, c2, c1, c3) VALUES (2, '2023-01-01', 20, 1);
-- In such a scenario (without PK), Q1's DAY1_RETENTION for '2023-01-01' would be ROUND((2/2), 2) = 1.00, while Q2's would be ROUND((1/2), 2) = 0.50.
-- However, with the PRIMARY KEY (c4, c2), such a scenario is impossible.

