counterexample:
-- No counterexample can be generated under standard SQL semantics as the queries are logically equivalent.
-- The problem statement implies a difference, which would require non-standard SQL behavior or a specific, unstated interpretation of NULLs or date handling.
-- For demonstration purposes, if we were to assume a hypothetical scenario where Q1's MIN/MAX ignores NULLs entirely (even if all are NULL) and Q2's NOT IN with UNKNOWN behaves differently, a counterexample might be possible. But this is not standard.

-- Example data where both queries return the same result (empty, as no c3 satisfies the condition):
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (1, 'ItemA', 100);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 1, 1, '2018-12-31', 10, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (1, 2, 2, '2019-04-01', 20, 2);

-- In this case:
-- Q1: MIN(c6) = '2018-12-31', MAX(c6) = '2019-04-01'. Neither satisfies the HAVING clause. Q1 returns empty.
-- Q2: The subquery `(SELECT DISTINCT table2.c3 FROM table2 WHERE NOT c6 BETWEEN ...)` will return {1} because '2018-12-31' and '2019-04-01' are outside the range. 
--     The main WHERE clause will then have `NOT table2.c3 IN {1}` which is false for c3=1. Q2 returns empty.

-- Example data where both queries return the same result (c3=2, 'ItemB'):
INSERT INTO table1 (c3, c4, c8) VALUES (2, 'ItemB', 200);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 1, 1, '2019-01-15', 10, 1);
INSERT INTO table2 (c3, c7, c1, c6, c5, c2) VALUES (2, 2, 2, '2019-02-28', 20, 2);

-- In this case:
-- Q1: MIN(c6) = '2019-01-15', MAX(c6) = '2019-02-28'. Both satisfy the HAVING clause. Q1 returns (2, 'ItemB').
-- Q2: The subquery `(SELECT DISTINCT table2.c3 FROM table2 WHERE NOT c6 BETWEEN ...)` will return an empty set {}. 
--     The main WHERE clause will then have `c6 BETWEEN ...` (true for both rows) AND `NOT table2.c3 IN {}` (true for c3=2). Q2 returns (2, 'ItemB').

